# .gitattributes -- line-ending pins for the files whose BYTES are evidence.
#
# T-L7.7. This repository's integrity story is SHA-256: `data/dataset_meta.json`
# and `data/lang/<corpus>/dataset_meta.json` record a digest for every split, and
# `code/test_language_data.py` / the correctness suite grade the files against
# those digests. A digest is over bytes, and on Windows `core.autocrlf=true` (the
# installer default -- it IS set on the machine this file was written on) rewrites
# line endings between the blob and the working copy. So for any text file whose
# digest is recorded, the EOL form is part of the evidence, and leaving it to a
# per-machine git setting means the published digests verify on some clones and
# fail on others.
#
# Both rules below were chosen so that NO BLOB CHANGES. They restate, as a
# repository-level fact, the form each recorded digest was actually computed over.
# Neither re-hashes anything and neither rewrites a tracked file's content
# (CLAUDE.md 6 / DN-2: the arithmetic dataset is published evidence and its
# recorded numbers may not move).

# --- Arithmetic dataset: digest recorded over the CRLF form ------------------
# MEASURED at T-L7.7, on the three files the arithmetic study publishes:
#
#   file                 blob EOL   sha256 in data/dataset_meta.json is of
#   data/train.jsonl     LF         CRLF  (d9ecb9a6...; the LF form is 656507ef...)
#   data/val.jsonl       LF         CRLF  (189dd7a8...; the LF form is b5c479c8...)
#   data/test.jsonl      LF         CRLF  (2a88633f...; the LF form is 61b851df...)
#
# They were generated and hashed on Windows with CRLF, then stored LF-normalized
# by autocrlf. Consequence before this file existed: a clone on Linux or macOS, or
# on Windows with `core.autocrlf=false|input`, materializes LF working copies and
# ALL THREE published digests fail. That failure is indistinguishable from a
# tampered dataset -- the worst possible way for a reproduction attempt to start,
# and it would have hit the first reviewer who cloned the artifact on Linux.
#
# `text eol=crlf` forces the working copy to CRLF on every platform, which is the
# form the recorded digest describes, while check-in still normalizes to LF -- and
# the blob is ALREADY LF, so this introduces no diff. Verified: `git status` stays
# clean and all three digests verify after adding this line.
#
# Do NOT "fix" this by re-hashing to the LF form. Those three digests are cited in
# the paper and in `runs/phaseB_*` provenance; changing them invalidates the
# published record to save one line here.
data/train.jsonl text eol=crlf
data/val.jsonl   text eol=crlf
data/test.jsonl  text eol=crlf

# --- Language tokenizers: digest recorded over the LF form -------------------
# The opposite pin, for the opposite reason. `tokenizer_sha256` in each language
# manifest is of the LF form (wikitext-2 2a714747..., wikitext-103 6a1f1477...),
# blob and working copy are both LF today -- but with autocrlf=true and no rule
# here, git says so out loud on every diff:
#
#   warning: in the working copy of 'data/lang/wikitext-103/tokenizer.json',
#            LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time Git touches it
#
# That is a live threat, not a cosmetic warning. `.gitignore` tracks tokenizer.json
# precisely because BPE merge order depends on the `tokenizers` version's
# tie-breaking, so a tokenizer that is not byte-identical makes every recorded
# language loss incomparable -- and a CRLF rewrite would do exactly that while
# `git diff` reported the file as unchanged.
#
# `-text` marks it binary: git never converts EOL in either direction, so the
# bytes on disk are the bytes in the blob on every platform and every autocrlf
# setting. Chosen over `text eol=lf` deliberately -- for a file whose bytes are
# load-bearing, the correct statement is "never touch this", not "convert it the
# way I happen to want today".
data/lang/**/tokenizer.json -text

# --- Everything else -------------------------------------------------------
# Left to git's defaults ON PURPOSE. In particular this file says nothing about
# `runs/**/*.json`: `config_hash` is computed by `run_context.canonical_json` over
# the PARSED object, not the file bytes, so it is EOL-immune -- and a blanket rule
# over `*.json` would be a redaction-shaped edit across 262 run records for no
# integrity gain (DN-1). Add a pin here only when a specific file's recorded
# digest is over its bytes, and record the measured EOL form when you do.
